function giveMePizza() {
  return "Pizza time!"
}

const giveMeChocolate = () => "Chocolate... now go to the gym.."

console.log(giveMePizza.prototype)
console.log(giveMeChocolate.prototype)


========


{ constructor: ... } undefined

Answer

Correct answer: D

Regular functions, such as giveMePizza, have a property prototype, which is an object (the object's prototype) with the property constructor. However, arrow functions, 
such as giveMeChocolate, do not have a prototype. Therefore, when attempting to access giveMeChocolate.prototype, undefined is returned.
